# App
CONFIG_DIR=config/
OPENAI_API_KEY=...
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=postgres
DB_PASSWORD=postgres
DB_NAME=nativespeaker

# Tests (e2e)
# JWT_API_KEY, not FIREBASE_API_KEY: pydantic-settings maps it onto `jwt.api_key`
# through the nested delimiter, and the old name is now merely an ignored extra key.
JWT_PROJECT_ID=...
JWT_API_KEY=...
FIREBASE_TEST_EMAIL=...
FIREBASE_TEST_PASSWORD=...

# --- The Google-linked session the upgrade e2e cases need ---
#
# These belong to a DEDICATED test Google account -- never a personal one. Nothing here
# is minted and nothing is signed by this project: each run redeems the stored refresh
# token for a fresh Google ID token, creates a throwaway anonymous Firebase user, links
# the Google account to it, and deletes that user afterwards so the account is free for
# the next run.
#
# The refresh token comes from ONE browser Google-consent flow, performed by hand, once.
# Request it with offline access (access_type=offline, prompt=consent) and include the
# `openid` scope alongside userinfo.email and userinfo.profile -- without `openid` a
# redemption returns no id_token and the link step has nothing to present.
#
# The client id below must ALSO be accepted as a Google sign-in provider for this Firebase
# project (Firebase Console -> Authentication -> Sign-in method -> Google), or the link
# call refuses the token. The client id and secret come from the same GCP Console ->
# APIs & Services -> Credentials entry the consent flow used.
#
# The refresh token below is the one real, long-lived secret in this block.
# It is revoked without any change to this repository: sign in to that test Google
# account, go to myaccount.google.com -> Security -> Your connections to third-party
# apps & services, and remove this app's access. Keep it in your gitignored .env only.
#
# All three must be present before the suite runs. Their absence is a broken environment,
# not a supported mode: the cases that use them FAIL, they do not skip. (The Admin
# credential block below documents a skip; this one deliberately does not.)
FIREBASE_TEST_GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN=...
FIREBASE_TEST_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...apps.googleusercontent.com
FIREBASE_TEST_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...

# --- Firebase Admin credential: Application Default Credentials, and nothing else ---
#
# Everything except completion mode runs without it: the service boots, prepare mode
# works, and a real completion fails closed as 503 verification_temporarily_unavailable.
# The e2e real-anonymous case (tests/e2e/test_create_user.py::TestTheRealAnonymousCompletion)
# SKIPS -- it does not fail -- when no credential is present, naming this route in the
# skip reason.
#
# ADC is the only route on projects whose org policy sets
# iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation -- as this one does, so no service-account key
# can be minted here at all. Point this at an ADC file kept OUTSIDE the repo, mode 600,
# or use a `gcloud auth application-default login` session. Google's own guidance ranks
# this above key files, which "are a security risk if not managed correctly".
#
# If your own gitignored .env still carries the service-account key variable this file
# used to document, delete that line: no code reads it any more, so it sits there
# looking load-bearing while doing nothing.
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/outside/this/repo/application_default_credentials.json

# --- Apple DeviceCheck credentials: the device gate on POST /auth/claim-anonymous-grant ---
#
# The key id and team id come from Apple Developer -> Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles
# -> Keys, on a key created with the DeviceCheck service enabled. Apple lets the .p8 be
# downloaded exactly once.
#
# The key path below points at that .p8 (a PEM-encoded EC P-256 private key) kept
# OUTSIDE this repository, mode 600. A path rather than the key itself, matching the Firebase
# ADC block above. These never go in config/config.yaml, which is tracked in git.
#
# Everything except the claim runs without them: the service boots, and a real claim fails
# closed as 503 verification_temporarily_unavailable. There is no bypass on any code path.
DEVICECHECK_KEY_ID=...
DEVICECHECK_TEAM_ID=...
DEVICECHECK_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=/absolute/path/outside/this/repo/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8

# --- App Store Server Notification settings: the route POST /webhooks/app-store ---
#
# The bundle id and the numeric Apple id are both read in App Store Connect, under
# Apps -> your app -> General -> App Information. The environment is not read there. It is
# a decision the deployer makes, and it must be written in a visible line.
#
# The environment has NO default. The two accepted values are sandbox and production.
# Apple signs sandbox notifications with the same chain it uses for production ones, and a
# sandbox purchase is free to anyone with a test build. An unstated environment would let
# sandbox traffic reach a production deployment.
#
# None of these three is a secret. A bundle id and an Apple id are public values. They stay
# out of config/config.yaml anyway: that file is init_settings, and it outranks every
# environment variable for a key it declares. A value written there could never be changed
# per environment. Only the public product map lives in that file.
#
# No root certificate path is listed. It defaults to config/certs/AppleRootCA-G3.cer, which
# is committed to this repository and is valid to 2039.
#
# Everything except the store notification runs without these: the service boots, it logs one
# app_store_configuration_absent warning, and the route fails closed as 503
# verification_temporarily_unavailable. Apple then retries on its own schedule.
APP_STORE_BUNDLE_ID=...
APP_STORE_APP_APPLE_ID=...
APP_STORE_ENVIRONMENT=...
